      "panels": [
        {
          "id": 22,
          "type": "text",
          "title": "TraceQL cheat sheet",
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 12,
            "w": 24,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 72
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "Cloudflare names the root span after the trigger (`POST`, `GET`, `queue`, `jsrpc`, `scheduled`), which is why Tempo search looks like an undifferentiated wall of `POST`. Filter on the Crosmos stage spans instead:\n\n| Want | TraceQL |\n| --- | --- |\n| Retrieval traces only | `{ name = \"api_stage.search_total\" }` |\n| Retrieval, slow ones | `{ name = \"api_stage.search_total\" && duration > 2s }` |\n| Client-visible search latency | `{ name = \"api.request_total\" && span.url.path = \"/api/v1/search\" }` |\n| Ingestion, per job | `{ name = \"ingestion.job_total\" }` |\n| Ingestion, per source | `{ name = \"ingestion.source_total\" }` |\n| Anything that failed | `{ span.crosmos.outcome = \"failed\" }` |\n| One stage | `{ span.crosmos.stage = \"rerank\" }` |\n| Provider HTTP calls | `{ name = \"fetch\" && span.url.path = \"/v1/embeddings\" }` |\n\nSpan naming: `api.request_total` wraps the whole request, `api_stage.*` are the API stages, `ingestion.job_total` / `ingestion.source_total` / `ingestion_stage.*` are the ingestion side. Every stage span also carries `crosmos.stage` and `crosmos.outcome`, plus `crosmos.input_count` / `crosmos.output_count` / `crosmos.transfer_bytes` where they apply.\n\nJump between logs and traces with the trace ID: `{service_name=~\"crosmos-(api|ingestion)-production\"} | trace_id=\"<id>\"`.\n\n### Three limits worth knowing\n\n1. TraceQL **metric** panels (`quantile_over_time`, `rate`, `count_over_time`) are capped at a 25-hour window by Grafana Cloud, so this dashboard defaults to 6h and those panels error out if you pick 2 days. Trace search tables have no such cap.\n2. Quantiles come from an exponential histogram, so at current traffic they snap to bucket boundaries (0.13s, 0.27s, 0.54s, 1.07s, 2.15s, 4.29s, 8.59s). Read them as ranges; use the trace tables or the log-derived latency panels on the logs dashboard when you need exact milliseconds.\n3. Empty buckets come back as `0` rather than null, so the stat tiles and the ranked bar gauge reduce with max or mean over the window instead of last value. A quiet minute would otherwise read as 0 ms.\n\nAPI-to-ingestion work is one connected trace when the API calls ingestion over the RPC service binding, so a single trace can show both workers. The queue backstop path arrives as its own `queue`-rooted trace instead; correlate those through `request_id` / job IDs in structured logs."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 23,
